.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt ED 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ed
.Nd line editor (sbase)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl p Ar string
.Op Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is sbase 0.1's
.Nm ed ,
built for ABSD with the C kit and one declared patch
.Pq Pa userland/patches/ed-absd.patch ;
see
.Xr sbase 7 .
It is the base userland's editor.
No screen editor can work in an ABSD session: the SSH terminal is a line
discipline in
.Nm sshd
with no raw mode, terminal type or window size
.Pq Xr absd-sh 1 .
.Nm
reads commands a line at a time from standard input, so it works there,
and as
.Ql ed FILE < script .
.Pp
Every editing command, undo, reads and writes, the
.Ql \&?
convention and the exit status are upstream's.
Errors are
.Ql \&? ,
with the reason after
.Ic h
(or every time after
.Ic H ) .
.Fl p Ar string
sets a prompt; without it there is none.
.Pp
What a person must know on ABSD:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Start it in a writable directory.
Its scratch file (the buffer's backing store) is
.Pa ./ed.XXXXXX
in the working directory; sessions start in read-only
.Pa / ,
so
.Ql cd /home/operator
or
.Ql cd /tmp/session-N
first.
Otherwise
.Nm
exits at once with status 1:
.Ql ed: cannot create scratch file ./ed.XXXXXX (start ed in a writable directory): <reason> .
The file is removed on
.Ic q ,
.Ic Q
or end of input.
.It
Leave with
.Ic w
then
.Ic q .
^D on an empty line closes the session's input for the whole session, and
.Nm
treats end of input as
.Ic q
without the modified-buffer warning: unsaved text is lost.
.It
^C loses the buffer.
No signal is delivered on ABSD; ^C ends the pipeline and
.Nm
with it, unsaved text is lost, and the scratch file
.Pa ./ed.XXXXXX
stays behind in the directory (remove it with
.Nm rm ;
in
.Pa /tmp/session-N
it goes with the session).
.It
No shell escapes.
.Ic !cmd
and
.Ic w !cmd
answer
.Ql \&? ;
.Ic h
shows
.Ql shell commands are not supported on this system
(no spawn right).
.Ic r !cmd
was never special in sbase 0.1: it reads a file named
.Pa !cmd .
.Ic !!
with no earlier command answers
.Ql no previous command
first, as upstream.
.El
.Sh UNSUPPORTED ON ABSD
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic !cmd , Ic w !cmd
.Ql \&? ;
.Ic h :
.Ql shell commands are not supported on this system ;
nothing runs; status 1 at exit.
.It scratch file
.Pa ./ed.XXXXXX
(or
.Ev TMPDIR ,
never set on ABSD); not creatable at start-up: status 1,
.Ql ed: cannot create scratch file ./ed.XXXXXX (start ed in a writable directory): <strerror> .
.It ^C, hang-up
no handler (no signals): the process ends, the buffer is lost,
.Pa ./ed.XXXXXX
remains;
.Pa ed.hup
is never written.
.It ^D on an empty line
closes the session's input;
.Nm
quits as on
.Ic q
without the modified warning: unsaved text is lost.
.It interactive prompt
only with
.Fl p Ar string ;
no line editing beyond
.Nm sshd Ns 's
erase.
.El
.Pp
Behaviour on ABSD is predicted until the guest qualification
.Pq Xr sbase 7 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 after any error, as upstream.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Write a file, in a terminal session:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ cd /home/operator
absd$ ed -p '* '
* a
int main(void) { return 0; }
\&.
* w hello.c
29
* q
.Ed
.Pp
Apply a script from the host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '1s/0/1/\enw\enq\en' | ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 \e
    'cd /home/operator && ed -s hello.c'
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Xr sed 1 ,
.Xr sbase 7
